<Base
  title="Research — FPGA quantum state discrimination, mid-circuit measurement, RL calibration | Neel R. Vora"
  description="Real-time quantum state discrimination on FPGAs, mid-circuit measurement for superconducting qubits, reinforcement learning for gate calibration, and crosstalk characterization at Berkeley Lab."
>
  <div class="shell hero">
    <span class="eyebrow">Research</span>
    <h1>Deciding fast enough to matter</h1>
    <p class="thesis">
      Quantum control is a latency problem before it is a machine learning
      problem. These are the threads I work on, and why each one exists.
    </p>
  </div>

  <section class="band" id="readout">
    <div class="shell prose">
      <span class="eyebrow">Thread 01 · Readout</span>
      <h2>Real-time quantum state discrimination on FPGA</h2>
      <p>
        Reading a qubit means firing a microwave pulse at its readout resonator
        and interpreting what comes back. The returned signal is noisy, and the
        two states you are trying to tell apart overlap. Classically you would
        integrate the signal, plot it on the IQ plane, and draw a boundary
        between two clouds of points.
      </p>
      <p>
        That works fine when the answer can arrive whenever. It does not work
        for mid-circuit measurement, where the result has to come back in time
        to change what the circuit does next. Conventional pipelines ship the
        raw measurement to a host processor, and the round trip runs into
        milliseconds — while the qubit itself holds coherence for microseconds.
        The decision arrives after the thing it was supposed to decide about is
        gone.
      </p>
      <div class="callout">
        QubiCML puts a compact multi-layer neural network on the control FPGA
        itself. Inference completes in 54 ns. Evaluated on in-house
        superconducting processors, it reached 98.5% average accuracy with a
        500 ns readout window — the first time ML-powered state discrimination
        ran on an RFSoC platform for mid-circuit measurement.
      </div>
      <p>
        The engineering constraint that shapes everything: the network has to
        fit in fabric alongside the existing control logic, hit timing closure,
        and produce an answer in fixed point. There is no room for a model that
        is merely accurate.
      </p>
      <ul class="datalist">
        <li><b>Platform</b> — Xilinx Zynq UltraScale+ RFSoC, ZCU216</li>
        <li><b>Stack</b> — QubiC open-source FPGA control system</li>
        <li><b>Papers</b> — IEEE QCE 2024; GLSVLSI 2026</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        <a href="/publications/qubicml-fpga-quantum-state-discrimination">QubiCML (IEEE QCE 2024)</a>
        ·
        <a href="/publications/ml-enabled-fpga-framework-mid-circuit-measurement">FPGA framework (GLSVLSI 2026)</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band" id="calibration">
    <div class="shell prose">
      <span class="eyebrow">Thread 02 · Control</span>
      <h2>Reinforcement learning for gate calibration</h2>
      <p>
        Superconducting gates drift. Keeping them accurate means repeatedly
        retuning pulse amplitude, phase and frequency — a process that is
        well-understood in its coarse stages and fiddly in its final ones,
        where the remaining error is small, correlated and hardware-specific.
      </p>
      <p>
        I have been treating that last stage as a control problem: an agent
        observes the calibration state, adjusts parameters, and is rewarded
        against a phase-estimation signal. The interesting difficulty is that
        the agent needs memory — what happened two adjustments ago matters —
        which standard implementations of continuous-control algorithms handle
        poorly.
      </p>
      <p>
        The work sits on top of the QubiC and QCal ecosystem, so an agent that
        works in simulation can be pointed at real hardware without a rewrite.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band" id="crosstalk">
    <div class="shell prose">
      <span class="eyebrow">Thread 03 · Characterization</span>
      <h2>Crosstalk and Hamiltonian learning</h2>
      <p>
        Operating one qubit disturbs its neighbours. That violation of
        locality is a coherent error, and coherent errors are the ones that
        accumulate rather than average out. Our approach fits a
        first-principles Hamiltonian to data from simultaneous Rabi
        experiments, then uses the fitted model to predict three- and
        four-qubit behaviour.
      </p>
      <p>
        The practical payoff is that you can study a multi-qubit circuit
        without running it, and characterize crosstalk across an entire chip —
        which turns out to be a useful input to processor design, not just
        error mitigation.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href="/publications/crosstalk-dynamics-hamiltonian-learning-rabi">Read the paper</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band" id="sensing">
    <div class="shell prose">
      <span class="eyebrow">Thread 04 · Earlier</span>
      <h2>Sensing under the same constraint</h2>
      <p>
        Before quantum, the same problem wore different clothes. DroneChase
        tracked a flying drone by fusing what it heard with what it saw, so
        the track survived occlusion and darkness — running on a Raspberry Pi
        mounted to a moving vehicle. EarSD detected seizure onset from signals
        measured behind the ear, in a device small enough to live inside
        headphones instead of a hospital.
      </p>
      <p>
        Both are the same engineering question as qubit readout: run a useful
        model where the power, latency and bandwidth budgets are already spent.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
